{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The general extension provisions affect people required to do a relevant act within a specified time, including people whose failure results from their own or their agent’s error or omission, or circumstances beyond their control; under s 224(2), the Registrar may extend time on their application.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s224",
          "quote": "(2) If, because of: (a) an error or omission by the person concerned or by his or her agent; or (b) circumstances beyond the control of the person concerned; a relevant act that is required by this Act to be done within a certain time is not, or cannot be, done within that time, the Registrar may, on application made by the person concerned in accordance with the regulations, extend the time for doing the act."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Registrar must extend time where the relevant act is not, or cannot be, done in time because of an error or omission by the Registrar, a Deputy Registrar, an employee, or a person providing or proposing to provide services for the Trade Marks Office.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s224",
          "quote": "(1) The Registrar must extend the time for doing a relevant act that is required by this Act to be done within a certain time if the act is not, or cannot be, done within that time because of an error or omission by: (a) the Registrar or a Deputy Registrar; or (b) an employee; or (c) a person providing, or proposing to provide, services for the benefit of the Trade Marks Office."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Applicants seeking extensions under s 224(2) or (3) must use an approved form and provide a declaration stating the supporting facts and, if the period has ended, why they did not apply before it ended.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMR1995/r21.25",
          "quote": "For the purposes of subsections 224(2) and (3) of the Act (which deal with extensions of time), an application for an extension of time for doing a relevant act: (a) must be in an approved form; and (b) must be accompanied by a declaration stating: (i) the facts on which the grounds specified in the application are based; and (ii) if the period for doing the relevant act has ended—the reason why the application was not made before the period ended."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Trade mark applicants approaching the lapsing date are affected by the Manual’s practice on Office delays: a free extension is not automatic, and free extensions within the initial extension period do not take that period beyond 21 months from the relevant report.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part15/1/1/1",
          "quote": "The Registrar may give consideration to such a request but note that approval of a free extension of time will not be the automatic result of any delay."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part15/1/1/1",
          "quote": "However, these free extensions of time will not extend the initial extension period past 21 months from the date of issue of the first report (or from the date of issue of a further adverse report raising new grounds for rejecting the application under paragraph 4.12(1)(a) of the Regulations)."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part15/1/1/1",
          "quote": "That is, once the 21 month date is reached, even if this happens because free extensions of time have been previously granted, the applicant must apply for any further extensions of time under the general provisions."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Applications under s 224(2) or (3) for more than three months affect both the extension applicant and potential opponents: the Registrar must publish the application, and a person may oppose its grant as prescribed.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s224",
          "quote": "(5) If an application is made under subsection (2) or (3) for an extension of time for more than 3 months, the Registrar must publish the application in accordance with the regulations."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s224(6)",
          "quote": "(6) A person may, as prescribed, oppose the granting of the application."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s opposition procedure, a person opposing an extension must file an approved-form notice within one month of publication, a period described as not extendible, and pay the relevant fee with the notice; the extension applicant receives a copy.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part15/6/6/2",
          "quote": "A notice of opposition to an extension of time under subsection 224(6) must be made in an approved form within one month (not extendible, see paragraph 21.28(2)(b)) of the date of publication (reg 21.20B). The relevant fee must also be paid with the notice of opposition. The Registrar will give a copy of the notice to the extension applicant (subreg 21.20B(2))."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual assigns processing responsibilities to TM&D Administration and the Hearings Support Unit, with the Registrar directing the parties according to the circumstances of each opposition to an extension of more than three months.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part15/6/6/2",
          "quote": "If the appropriate fee has been paid, the notice of opposition will be entered into the system by TM&D Administration; this will prevent the system from processing the extension of time. The notice will then be forwarded electronically to the Hearings Support Unit for further action."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part15/6/6/2",
          "quote": "In accordance with reg 21.20D, the procedures specified for oppositions to extensions of time of more than three months are to be decided by the Registrar according to the individual circumstances of each opposition. The Registrar will direct the parties accordingly."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Parties filing evidence in opposition proceedings are affected by the Manual’s extension requirements: absent exceptional circumstances, they must show all reasonable efforts to comply with filing requirements and prompt and diligent conduct throughout.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part51/2#1~1",
          "quote": "There are strict requirements for obtaining extensions of time for filing evidence. Absent exceptional circumstances, parties must show that they have made all reasonable efforts to comply with all filing requirements, and that they have acted promptly and diligently at all times to ensure the evidence is filed within the period."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "A person seeking a late award of costs or filing a late bill of costs is affected by the Manual’s requirement to obtain an extension under s 224, using an approved form accompanied by the appropriate fee and a supporting declaration.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part55/3/3/4",
          "quote": "A late application for an award of costs, or a bill of costs that has been filed outside the time limit under reg 21.12, can only be considered if the person concerned is granted an extension of time under s 224. Such an application for extension must be in an approved form and must be filed together with the appropriate fee and a supporting declaration (reg 21.25)."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Parties seeking to recover extension fees through a costs award are affected by the Manual’s practice that fees for extending evidence-service time or filing late evidence are rarely recoverable, with recovery confined to the stated circumstance of the other party causing the need for further time.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part55/4/4/1~2",
          "quote": "Fees required for extending the time for serving evidence, or for filing late evidence, in the proceedings will rarely be covered by an award of costs."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part55/4/4/1~2",
          "quote": "Such fees could only be claimed in the unlikely circumstance that the need for further time was the result of some action (or lack of action) by the other party."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The listed hearing-cost rules also affect parties attending hearings, their agents, and people summoned as expert or other witnesses.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part55/4/4/1~2",
          "quote": "Item 2 covers expenses associated with attendance at the hearing by a party to the proceedings, or that party’s agent."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part55/4/4/1~2",
          "quote": "Part 2, Division 2 of Schedule 8 covers the allowances which must be paid to a person summoned to appear at the hearing as an expert witness or otherwise."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied material does not establish the complete scope of every listed fee, filing or lapsing rule: it lists, but does not reproduce, s 223 and regulations 4.12, 21.4 and 21.23, so this account of affected people and roles may not be complete.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
